        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            For the way most people use a portable generator — outdoors, with extension cords running from its own receptacles — no ground rod is required. The code permits the frame to serve as the grounding means because the protective path is bonding, not earth, and that bonding is done at the factory. Read your manual to confirm, because it overrides any general guidance including this page.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Connecting to house wiring is a genuinely different problem. The neutral bonding of your generator has to match the transfer switch type, or you end up with return current on grounding conductors or a system with no reference at all, neither of which is visible from outside. Find out whether your machine is bonded or floating neutral, write it on a label, and have an electrician do the installation.
          </p>
        </div>

